/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 206 0 R
/T (Below, we describe the historical development as well as the contemporary institutional character and role of key regional organizations in different world regions. As we show, the bilateral relations of key states in each region, combined with the role of great powers, are central factors in shaping regional cooperation. In both Africa and Asia, colonial ties and the role of great powers helped shape the timing as well as the degree of regional cooperation. For example, after 1945 the US promoted and helped invest in regional integration in Europe, notably via the Marshall Plan and active participation in the North Atlantic Treaty Organization \(NATO\). In Asia, by contrast, the US opted for bilateral relations with key allies to shape developments there \(Kahler, 2017\).)
/E (Below, we describe the historical development as well as the contemporary institutional character and role of key regional organizations in different world regions. As we show, the bilateral relations of key states in each region, combined with the role of great powers, are central factors in shaping regional cooperation. In both Africa and Asia, colonial ties and the role of great powers helped shape the timing as well as the degree of regional cooperation. For example, after 1945 the US promoted and helped invest in regional integration in Europe, notably via the Marshall Plan and active participation in the North Atlantic Treaty Organization \(NATO\). In Asia, by contrast, the US opted for bilateral relations with key allies to shape developments there \(Kahler, 2017\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
