363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 208 0 R
/T (The Americas have a complex network of international organizations, with varying regional and topical focuses. Some organizations cover the whole continent \(the Organization of American States \(OAS\)\), while others focus on segmented areas, such as Latin America \(Latin American Integration Association \(ALADI\)\), South America \(Union of South American Nations \(UNASUR\)\), Central America \(Central American Integration System \(SICA\)\) or the Caribbean Community \(CARICOM\). Some organizations have a sub-regional focus, such as the Mercosur \(Southern Common Market\) and the Andean Community, others, have an interregional scope, such as the Pacific Alliance. Regarding mandates and issue areas, some are more focused on peace and security \(OAS, historically\), others on trade \(Mercosur, NAFTA/USMCA\), and others on political aspects \(UNASUR\), Forum for the Progress and Integration of South America \(PROSUR\). In contrast with the European region, in which the EU has been growing through a more cumulative process of enlargement, regionalism in the Americas has advanced in not so continuous waves \(Rosenthal, 1991\). This resulted in two different, even if interconnected, processes occurring simultaneously: segmented and overlapping regionalism \(Malamud & Gardini, 2012\). Following a chronological overview, it is possible to identify three general waves of institutionalized regionalism, with the creation of region organizations marked by two main issues: security and economic integration.)
/E (The Americas have a complex network of international organizations, with varying regional and topical focuses. Some organizations cover the whole continent \(the Organization of American States \(OAS\)\), while others focus on segmented areas, such as Latin America \(Latin American Integration Association \(ALADI\)\), South America \(Union of South American Nations \(UNASUR\)\), Central America \(Central American Integration System \(SICA\)\) or the Caribbean Community \(CARICOM\). Some organizations have a sub-regional focus, such as the Mercosur \(Southern Common Market\) and the Andean Community, others, have an interregional scope, such as the Pacific Alliance. Regarding mandates and issue areas, some are more focused on peace and security \(OAS, historically\), others on trade \(Mercosur, NAFTA/USMCA\), and others on political aspects \(UNASUR\), Forum for the Progress and Integration of South America \(PROSUR\). In contrast with the European region, in which the EU has been growing through a more cumulative process of enlargement, regionalism in the Americas has advanced in not so continuous waves \(Rosenthal, 1991\). This resulted in two different, even if interconnected, processes occurring simultaneously: segmented and overlapping regionalism \(Malamud & Gardini, 2012\). Following a chronological overview, it is possible to identify three general waves of institutionalized regionalism, with the creation of region organizations marked by two main issues: security and economic integration.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 208 0 R
/T (The first wave resulted from historical processes driven by US security strategy. The Inter-American Treaty of Reciprocal Assistance \(TIAR or Rio Pact\), firmed in 1942 and still in effect, was the expression of the influence exerted by the US on the continent, rooted in the 19th century Monroe Doctrine and the Pan-American conferences \(Bianculli, 2016\). In 1948, the Organization of American States \(OAS\) was also built as a strengthened and institutionalized body for the promotion of peace in the continent under US hegemony \(Nolte, 2018\). The OAS creation met the traditional interest of the Latin American countries in formalizing the principle of non-intervention, against possible actions of great powers \(including the US\) \(Herz, Hoffman & Tabak, 2015\). The organization reached universal, regional membership of 35 states in the early 1990s. Over time, OAS also expanded its mandate to include economic development, human rights[1], and the defense of democracy.)
/E (The first wave resulted from historical processes driven by US security strategy. The Inter-American Treaty of Reciprocal Assistance \(TIAR or Rio Pact\), firmed in 1942 and still in effect, was the expression of the influence exerted by the US on the continent, rooted in the 19th century Monroe Doctrine and the Pan-American conferences \(Bianculli, 2016\). In 1948, the Organization of American States \(OAS\) was also built as a strengthened and institutionalized body for the promotion of peace in the continent under US hegemony \(Nolte, 2018\). The OAS creation met the traditional interest of the Latin American countries in formalizing the principle of non-intervention, against possible actions of great powers \(including the US\) \(Herz, Hoffman & Tabak, 2015\). The organization reached universal, regional membership of 35 states in the early 1990s. Over time, OAS also expanded its mandate to include economic development, human rights[1], and the defense of democracy.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
