/T (Since 2020, 16 military coups have led to takeovers in Burkina Faso, Chad, Gabon, Guinea, Niger, Mali, and Sudan. Groups like Boko Haram and Al-Shabaab continue to pose major security threats. The protracted nature of armed conflicts and the limited effectiveness of multilateral peacekeeping have eroded public trust in regional and international institutions. In response, many African states are increasingly turning to alternative security arrangements. Military governments in West Africa have ceased to cooperate in established regional organizations such as ECOWAS and started setting up their own institutions weakening existing multilateral institutions.   )
/E (Since 2020, 16 military coups have led to takeovers in Burkina Faso, Chad, Gabon, Guinea, Niger, Mali, and Sudan. Groups like Boko Haram and Al-Shabaab continue to pose major security threats. The protracted nature of armed conflicts and the limited effectiveness of multilateral peacekeeping have eroded public trust in regional and international institutions. In response, many African states are increasingly turning to alternative security arrangements. Military governments in West Africa have ceased to cooperate in established regional organizations such as ECOWAS and started setting up their own institutions weakening existing multilateral institutions.   )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
