562 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 223 0 R
/T (Finally, in Europe, Figure 7 display trust levels in both member states and candidate states where more than one WVS wave of surveys was conducted. Overall. Eastern and Central Europe countries exhibit more positive views toward the EU: Bulgaria, the Czech Republic, Hungary, Romania and Slovakia have around 50% of their respondents expressing confidence in the EU. In contrast, trust in the EU has declined over time in Poland and Slovenia. A trend of increasing confidence can be observed also in Western European \(Germany, Spain and the Netherlands\) and Northern European countries \(Finland and Sweden\).)
/E (Finally, in Europe, Figure 7 display trust levels in both member states and candidate states where more than one WVS wave of surveys was conducted. Overall. Eastern and Central Europe countries exhibit more positive views toward the EU: Bulgaria, the Czech Republic, Hungary, Romania and Slovakia have around 50% of their respondents expressing confidence in the EU. In contrast, trust in the EU has declined over time in Poland and Slovenia. A trend of increasing confidence can be observed also in Western European \(Germany, Spain and the Netherlands\) and Northern European countries \(Finland and Sweden\).)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 223 0 R
/T (Confidence in the EU is mixed in candidate states. In the Balkans, Albania and Bosnia and Herzegovina maintained high levels of trust in the EU until the mid-2000s, while respondents from North Macedonia and Serbia exhibit distrust levels exceeding 60%, a perspective also mirrored in Turkey. In contrast, Georgia and Moldova show high levels of confidence in the institution. Ukraine, tough with less than 50% of trust, has shown an increasing trend over time. Finally, the UK \(a former EU state rather than a candidate\) respondents experienced a rise in trust in the EU between the 2005-2009 and 2017-2022.)
/E (Confidence in the EU is mixed in candidate states. In the Balkans, Albania and Bosnia and Herzegovina maintained high levels of trust in the EU until the mid-2000s, while respondents from North Macedonia and Serbia exhibit distrust levels exceeding 60%, a perspective also mirrored in Turkey. In contrast, Georgia and Moldova show high levels of confidence in the institution. Ukraine, tough with less than 50% of trust, has shown an increasing trend over time. Finally, the UK \(a former EU state rather than a candidate\) respondents experienced a rise in trust in the EU between the 2005-2009 and 2017-2022.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
