/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 206 0 R
/T (The rise of regionalism is not solely demand-driven, however, but also influenced by the strategies of major powers like the United States, the EU, and China. For example, the US has in the past used regional trade agreements to embed its preferred rules rather than to operate via the WTO \(Henning, 2020\); the EU has sought to export its model of regional integration to shape the functioning of the AU \(Haastrup, 2013\), and China, sometimes in collaboration with Russia, is actively establishing new regional arrangements, such as the Shanghai Cooperation Organization \(SCO\) to vest stronger influence in the region and keep other great powers at bay \(Song, 2013\).)
/E (The rise of regionalism is not solely demand-driven, however, but also influenced by the strategies of major powers like the United States, the EU, and China. For example, the US has in the past used regional trade agreements to embed its preferred rules rather than to operate via the WTO \(Henning, 2020\); the EU has sought to export its model of regional integration to shape the functioning of the AU \(Haastrup, 2013\), and China, sometimes in collaboration with Russia, is actively establishing new regional arrangements, such as the Shanghai Cooperation Organization \(SCO\) to vest stronger influence in the region and keep other great powers at bay \(Song, 2013\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
>>
