/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 229 0 R
/T (The tensions underpinning the EU-AU relationship are becoming more impactful for the EU as the AU and African countries now have the opportunity to diversify their relations with EU competitors such as China and Russia, thus gaining a leverage and room for maneuver that they did not have before \(Carbone, 2023\). More generally, changes in the international context are re-shaping the possibilities of this interregional relation. Abrahamsen and Chimhandamba \(2023\) point to the fact that the democratization of African states in the 1990s enabled their convergence with the post-cold war liberal order and the birth of an AU that enshrined liberal norms of democracy, human rights and the rule of law in its founding status and institutions. However, the current challenge of the liberal international order both outside and also inside the West, is providing African states with new options that speak to a pan-African view challenging the domination of the West, as well as new economic and investment opportunities, and political alliances among a broader pool of partners. This situation has enabled African states to become more autonomous in their economic and political strategies, avoiding for instance the political conditionalities imposed by the EU and reducing their dependence on former colonial powers \(Abrahamsen & Chimhandamba, 2023; Carbone, 2023; Shiferaw & Di Ciommo, 2023\).)
/E (The tensions underpinning the EU-AU relationship are becoming more impactful for the EU as the AU and African countries now have the opportunity to diversify their relations with EU competitors such as China and Russia, thus gaining a leverage and room for maneuver that they did not have before \(Carbone, 2023\). More generally, changes in the international context are re-shaping the possibilities of this interregional relation. Abrahamsen and Chimhandamba \(2023\) point to the fact that the democratization of African states in the 1990s enabled their convergence with the post-cold war liberal order and the birth of an AU that enshrined liberal norms of democracy, human rights and the rule of law in its founding status and institutions. However, the current challenge of the liberal international order both outside and also inside the West, is providing African states with new options that speak to a pan-African view challenging the domination of the West, as well as new economic and investment opportunities, and political alliances among a broader pool of partners. This situation has enabled African states to become more autonomous in their economic and political strategies, avoiding for instance the political conditionalities imposed by the EU and reducing their dependence on former colonial powers \(Abrahamsen & Chimhandamba, 2023; Carbone, 2023; Shiferaw & Di Ciommo, 2023\).)
/Pg 34 0 R
>>
endobj
